---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import candidateCoverage from "../../data/candidate-coverage.json";
import peopleData from "../../data/people.json";

const peopleCount = peopleData.length.toLocaleString();
const candidateCount = candidateCoverage.total_candidates.toLocaleString();
const backlogCount = candidateCoverage.queued_backlog.toLocaleString();
---

<Base title="About — Look Sideways">
  <section class="hero" style="padding-bottom:24px;">
    <h1>About this project</h1>
    <p class="lede">An uncapped public-evidence coverage project with {candidateCount} discovered candidates and {peopleCount} published profiles.</p>
  </section>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>
      A research beta mapping people who reached a material professional, commercial, creative,
      athletic, or research milestone early in their careers. The public model keeps their
      conditions in three places: what the person brought, what they were handed, and what
      surrounded them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Perseverance appears only when sources document repeated work, setbacks, recovery, or
      sustained practice. Luck remains visible and unscored. The early milestone determines
      inclusion; outcome reach summarizes documented recognition through the data cutoff. The
      archive includes global icons, field leaders, and professionally distinctive paths. The
      working target is the top 0.001% of each exact-age global cohort, but that calibration remains
      under methodology review. See <a href="/coverage/">the live coverage funnel</a> and
      <a href="/insights/">how the evidence model works</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What it is not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Not a claim of complete capture: private, local, and poorly indexed outcomes remain hard to discover.</li>
      <li>Not representative of the full population; the corpus intentionally contains successful outliers.</li>
      <li>Not a causal estimate of which advantages produce success.</li>
      <li>Not a ranking of human worth or talent.</li>
      <li>Not a predictor of individual outcomes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Two evidence layers</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Biographical layer</strong> — names, milestones, age assignments, early-history
      summaries, family context, and source URLs — intended to summarize public biographical
      evidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Annotation layer</strong> — leverage engines and numeric scores — are analyst
      interpretations applied through a common rubric. They are hypotheses about acceleration
      mechanisms, not measurements.
    </p>

    <h2>Source & release terms</h2>
    <p>
      This research build includes {peopleCount} published people born in or after 1950 and keeps
      {backlogCount} discovered candidates visible for research or publication. Release terms for the
      original annotations and scripts are still under review; the repository does not yet grant
      the suggested CC BY 4.0 or MIT licences. Third-party sources retain their own terms.
      See <a href="https://github.com/Significant-Hobbies/what-it-takes-to-win">GitHub</a> for source.
    </p>

    <h2>Building the site locally</h2>
    <p>
      <code>npm install &amp;&amp; npm run dev</code> — Astro dev server on
      <code>http://localhost:4321</code>. Build with <code>npm run build</code>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
